        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "00e4afe1-4ceb-5e3e-ab32-55094b2530e9",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Annie Dillard, Gabriel García Márquez\n- Works: Pilgrim at Tinker Creek, The Maytrees\n- Awards: Pulitzer Prize for General Nonfiction, Neustadt International Prize for Literature, Nobel Prize in Literature\n- Places: Latin America, Provincetown, Massachusetts\n\nFacts:\n- Annie Dillard's 1974 work \"Pilgrim at Tinker Creek\" won the 1975 Pulitzer Prize for General Nonfiction.\n- Gabriel José de la Concordia García Márquez was born on 6 March 1927 and became a Colombian novelist, short-story writer, screenwriter, and journalist known affectionately as Gabo or Gabito throughout Latin America.\n- Gabriel García Márquez was considered one of the most significant authors of the 20th century and one of the best writers in the Spanish language.\n- Gabriel García Márquez received the 1972 Neustadt International Prize for Literature.\n- Annie Dillard's novel \"The Maytrees\" is her second novel and is a fictional account of the lives of Toby and Lou Maytree in Provincetown, Massachusetts, from courtship to old age.\n- Gabriel García Márquez received the 1982 Nobel Prize in Literature.\n- Gabriel García Márquez died on 17 April 2014.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
